@*
    The board, in the same fixed 728x728 space the desktop app draws in — so the
    geometry constants below are the same numbers, and the SVG viewBox does the
    scaling that a Viewbox does there.

    Hover works without a mode switch, as on the desktop: every groove between two
    squares is its own target, so which way the wall lies follows from where the
    cursor is rather than from a toggle.

    A finger is the same pointer with the press held down. The whole board is driven by
    pointer events rather than by mouse events and clicks, which is what lets one set of
    rules serve both: pressing aims, moving the pointer re-aims, and lifting plays what is
    aimed at. With a mouse that is exactly the hover-and-click it always was — the aim
    follows the cursor and the release commits it. With a finger the press is the hover:
    the wall appears under the thumb with what it costs written under the board, the aim
    follows the thumb as it slides, and the move is not played until the thumb comes off.
    Sliding off the board and lifting there plays nothing, which is how a press is taken
    back. Nothing is a mode and nothing is a second gesture: placing a wall is press, look,
    lift, and moving a pawn is the same press, look, lift.

    Two things make that work rather than merely describe it. The board asks the page to
    let go of the implicit pointer capture a touch press takes (see `freeTheBoard` in
    page.js), or the elements the thumb slides onto would never hear about it; and on a
    coarse pointer the grooves are given a fair share of the pitch instead of the sliver
    that is enough for a mouse — see `Bleed`.
*@

@* A smaller game is played on a centred square of the same grid, so the geometry below
   never changes — the view simply looks at less of it. *@
@* `turned` is not decoration: everything below is drawn inside a group that is rotated a
   half turn for the second seat, and a pawn told to rise would then dip instead. The
   class carries the sign so the arc stays a rise on the screen either way.

   `reading` says the board has stopped being a made object and become a chart. It is a
   state and not a layer: a bevelled tile and a stain are the same pixel, and eighty-one
   translucent rects laid over the board to have both would be a diagram pretending to be
   material. For as long as the reading is up, the tiles are the reading.

   `past` says this position is being looked at rather than played. Nothing that arrives
   is news any more: a wall stepped back to and forward again is not being placed, and a
   board that re-announces every move on the way through forty of them is a board you are
   fighting. Only the pawns keep moving, because that is what stepping through a game is
   for.

   `carved` is the board as a made object — lit, bevelled, walls standing proud of their
   grooves. It is the second way of drawing the same board and not the default one: the
   flat board is what this game is, and every rule that gives it a material lives under
   this class in the stylesheet so that a viewer with the switch off gets none of it.

   The keydown is on the board itself rather than on the document, because by the time it
   fires something inside the board is focused and the keys are the board's own.

   The press and the release are on the board and not on the two hundred targets inside it.
   By the time either arrives the aim is already right — a pointer reports the target it is
   over before it reports being pressed there, and it goes on reporting as it moves — so
   the targets only have to say where the pointer is, and the board says what to do about
   it. That is one handler per target instead of three, and it is also what makes a press
   that starts on one square and lifts on another play the one it lifted on. *@
<svg class="board @(Carved ? "carved" : "") @(Flipped ? "turned" : "") @(Reading ? "reading" : "") @(Reviewing ? "past" : "")" viewBox="@ViewBox" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet" role="group" aria-label="Quoridor board"
     @onpointerdown="Press" @onpointerup="Release" @onpointercancel="Abandon" @onpointerleave="LeaveBoard" @onkeydown="OnKeyDown">

    @* A board is a physical thing; a flat rectangle of one colour is not. Every gradient
       here is painted once — no animation, nothing per frame. *@
    <defs>
        @* The rim, which is the raised part of the tray and so the part nearest the lamp. *@
        <linearGradient id="board-sheen" x1="0" y1="0" x2="0" y2="1">
            <stop offset="0%" stop-color="var(--board-top)" />
            <stop offset="100%" stop-color="var(--board-bottom)" />
        </linearGradient>

        @* The field is sunk into the rim, and the dark of a recess collects in a narrow
           band directly under its top edge rather than washing evenly down the whole. *@
        <linearGradient id="field-well" x1="0" y1="0" x2="0" y2="1">
            <stop offset="0%" stop-color="var(--field-deep)" />
            <stop offset="10%" stop-color="var(--field)" />
            <stop offset="100%" stop-color="var(--field)" />
        </linearGradient>

        @* Every tile is bevelled by its own fill: lit along the top edge, shaded along the
           bottom, flat between. Object-bound, so this one gradient bevels all eighty-one
           squares at whatever size they are drawn, and adds no element to the board. *@
        <linearGradient id="tile-face" x1="0" y1="0" x2="0" y2="1" gradientTransform="@TurnedPaint">
            <stop offset="0%" stop-color="var(--tile-lit)" />
            <stop offset="9%" stop-color="var(--cell)" />
            <stop offset="88%" stop-color="var(--cell)" />
            <stop offset="100%" stop-color="var(--tile-shade)" />
        </linearGradient>

        @* The lamp itself, running corner to corner of the view. In user space rather than
           object space so it crosses the board once, instead of repeating inside every
           shape it is laid over. The stops are coloured from CSS because how much light a
           room has differs between the themes and only stop-opacity can say so. *@
        <linearGradient id="board-light" gradientUnits="userSpaceOnUse"
                        x1="@ViewX" y1="@ViewY" x2="@(ViewX + ViewSize)" y2="@(ViewY + ViewSize)">
            <stop class="lit" offset="0%" />
            <stop class="clear" offset="50%" />
            <stop class="shade" offset="100%" />
        </linearGradient>

        @* A piece with a top rather than a disc cut out of paper: the light collects up
           and to the left of it and falls away to the far edge. One gradient serves both
           players and both themes, because how a piece is lit belongs to the room and
           not to whoever owns it. *@
        <radialGradient id="dome" cx="50%" cy="50%" r="62%" fx="33%" fy="27%" gradientTransform="@TurnedPaint">
            <stop offset="0%" stop-color="var(--lit)" stop-opacity="0.34" />
            <stop offset="52%" stop-color="var(--lit)" stop-opacity="0" />
            <stop offset="100%" stop-color="var(--shade)" stop-opacity="0.26" />
        </radialGradient>

        @* What a piece standing on the board puts underneath itself. A gradient and not a
           blur: a filter would be re-rasterised on every frame of the slide, and the
           slide is the one thing on this board that actually moves. *@
        <radialGradient id="contact">
            <stop offset="0%" stop-color="var(--shadow)" stop-opacity="0.55" />
            <stop offset="100%" stop-color="var(--shadow)" stop-opacity="0" />
        </radialGradient>

        @* Looking down into a square that is not there. The near wall of the opening is in
           its own shadow and the far one is the part the lamp still reaches, so this runs
           the same way across the board as every other gradient here. *@
        <linearGradient id="pit-wall" x1="0" y1="0" x2="1" y2="1" gradientTransform="@TurnedPaint">
            <stop offset="0%" stop-color="var(--pit)" />
            <stop offset="100%" stop-color="var(--pit-lit)" />
        </linearGradient>

        @* And down through a portal, which is an opening that is not a well: what is at the
           bottom of it is the far mouth, so the light that comes back up the shaft is the
           pair's own and the dark is in the middle rather than at the edges.

           One gradient per pair and not one per mouth. A gradient cannot be re-tinted by
           whatever refers to it — its stops read the custom properties of where it is
           defined, not of where it is used — and there are at most two pairs on any board,
           so two of them is the whole of the cost. The stops are coloured from CSS, like
           the lamp's, because which two hues these are is a decision about the theme. *@
        <radialGradient id="portal-a">
            <stop class="deep" offset="0%" />
            <stop class="glow" offset="100%" />
        </radialGradient>

        <radialGradient id="portal-b">
            <stop class="deep" offset="0%" />
            <stop class="glow" offset="100%" />
        </radialGradient>
    </defs>

    @* The tray: a raised rim with the field recessed into it. The coordinate labels below
       already sit inside the rim band, which is the whole point of the inset — they stop
       floating beside the board and start reading as printed on its frame. *@
    <rect class="rim" x="@ViewX" y="@ViewY" width="@ViewSize" height="@ViewSize" rx="6" />
    <rect class="field" x="@(ViewX + Frame)" y="@(ViewY + Frame)"
          width="@(ViewSize - Frame * 2)" height="@(ViewSize - Frame * 2)" rx="4" />

    @* Razor claims <text> for its own block syntax, so the coordinate labels are the
       one part of the board that has to be emitted through the render tree directly. *@
    @Coordinates

    @* Taking the second seat turns the board around so that seat is still the near
       edge. Rotating the whole group means every coordinate below — including the hit
       targets, which keep reporting the squares they actually are — stays untouched.
       Only the labels sit outside it, since text upside down is no use to anyone. *@
    <g transform="@(Flipped ? "rotate(180 364 364)" : "")">

    @* Only the squares in the game are drawn. On a smaller board the ring around it
       would otherwise poke into view, since the frame is a clean margin around the
       playable square and the ring's squares start half a cell outside it. *@
    @{ int opened = 0; }
    @for (int row = From; row <= Last; row++)
    {
        for (int col = From; col <= Last; col++)
        {
            int cell = Board.Index(row, col);
            bool hole = (Holes & Board.Bit(cell)) != 0;

            @* Only the gaps are keyed on the game. A plain square is the same square in
               every game and rebuilding it would buy nothing; a gap is what this board
               has that the last one did not, so it gets a fresh element and opens. They
               open in turn rather than at once — a dozen things moving together read as
               one flicker, and the point is to be able to count them.

               A gap is an opening you can see down into: the mouth carries the wall you
               are looking past and the floor is the bottom of it. The delay rides on the
               group as a custom property because animation-delay does not inherit and the
               two rects have to sink together. *@
            @if (hole)
            {
                <g @key="(Generation, row, col)" class="pit"
                   style="--delay: @(Stagger(opened++, HoleCount, 0, 90))ms">
                    <rect class="mouth" x="@Origin(col)" y="@Origin(row)"
                          width="@Cell" height="@Cell" rx="3" />
                    <rect class="floor" x="@(Origin(col) + PitDepth)" y="@(Origin(row) + PitDepth)"
                          width="@(Cell - PitDepth * 2)" height="@(Cell - PitDepth * 2)" rx="2" />
                </g>
            }
            else
            {
                @* Outside reading mode the class and the style are both empty and this is
                   the plain bevelled tile. Inside it, the same rect is the chart. *@
                <rect @key="(0, row, col)" class="cell @Territory(cell)" style="@Stain(cell)"
                      x="@Origin(col)" y="@Origin(row)" width="@Cell" height="@Cell" rx="3" />
            }
        }
    }

    @* Each player's target row: a wash across the squares and a finish line on the edge. *@
    @for (int player = 0; player < 2; player++)
    {
        int goal = State.GoalRow(player);
        string accent = player == 0 ? "p0" : "p1";

        for (int col = From; col <= Last; col++)
        {
            <rect class="goal @accent" x="@Origin(col)" y="@Origin(goal)" width="@Cell" height="@Cell" rx="3" />
        }

        @* Winning is currently a card that arrives over the move that won. The line the
           pawn has just crossed floods outward from the column it crossed in, so the move
           is seen before anything is drawn on top of it. This is the one animation in the
           build that may run long: the game is over and nothing is thinking. *@
        bool won = State.Winner == player;

        <rect class="finish @accent @(won ? "won" : "")" style="@(won ? Crossing(player) : null)"
              x="@(Origin(From))" y="@(goal == From ? Origin(goal) : Origin(goal) + Cell - 3)"
              width="@(Span * Cell + (Span - 1) * Gap)" height="3" />
    }

    @* The portal mouths, bored through the board where the gaps are cut out of it — the
       same wall of an opening lit the same way, because the board should have one way of
       saying "this square is not solid" and then one way of saying what is under it. A gap
       has a floor; a mouth has a throat with the far end's light coming up it.

       Both mouths of a pair are drawn in one ink, and the only thing a player wants from a
       mouth is where it comes out: with two pairs on the board a shared colour answers that
       at a glance, where two labels or a line ruled across the middle would not. Neither
       ink is a player's, since a mouth belongs to the board rather than to a side.

       Wide enough that a pawn standing in one sits inside it rather than on top of it, so
       the square goes on saying where it leads while it is occupied — which is exactly the
       moment the question is being asked.

       They open with the gaps rather than with the prizes: both are what this board has
       that the last one did not, and the ground is read before what is lying on it. *@
    @{ int bored = 0; List<(int Cell, int Pair)> mouths = Mouths(); }
    @foreach ((int cell, int pair) in mouths)
    {
        <g @key="@($"portal-{Generation}-{cell}")" class="portal @(pair == 0 ? "pair-a" : "pair-b")"
           style="--delay: @(Stagger(bored++, mouths.Count, 30, 60))ms"
           transform="translate(@Centre(Board.ColOf(cell)) @Centre(Board.RowOf(cell)))">
            @* The wall of the opening, what is down it, the edge where the board's surface
               breaks, and one ring further down — which is what makes the last of them read
               as depth rather than as a coloured disc lying on the square.

               `lip` rather than `rim` because the tray of the board is already the board's
               rim, and `.board .rim` would otherwise paint the edge of every mouth in the
               tray's own sheen. *@
            <circle class="bore" r="24" />
            <circle class="throat" r="17" />
            <circle class="lip" r="24" />
            <circle class="iris" r="9" />
        </g>
    }

    @* A placed wall is a solid piece standing proud of its groove, and a previewed one is
       a flat shape lying in it — the one difference this board teaches without a word of
       explanation. The shadow falls away from the lamp, the side face fills the slot the
       preview would occupy, and the top face is lifted toward the lamp by Lift. Offsets
       do the whole of the extrusion: a blur here would be re-rasterised on every redraw
       of the board, and there are up to twenty of these.

       Every one of those offsets carries the sign in Lamp, which is what keeps the light
       above and to the left of the piece on the board that has been turned around — see
       there for why a negation is the whole of the correction. *@
    @foreach ((MoveKind kind, int row, int col, int owner) in PlacedWalls())
    {
        bool flat = kind == MoveKind.HorizontalWall;

        int x = WallX(kind, col);
        int y = WallY(kind, row);
        int width = flat ? WallLength : Gap;
        int height = flat ? Gap : WallLength;

        <g class="wall @(owner == 0 ? "p0" : "p1")">
            <rect class="cast" rx="2" x="@(x + Lift * Lamp)" y="@(y + (Lift + 1) * Lamp)" width="@width" height="@height" />
            <rect class="side" rx="2" x="@x" y="@y" width="@width" height="@height" />
            <rect class="top" rx="2" x="@(x - Lift * Lamp)" y="@(y - Lift * Lamp)" width="@width" height="@height" />
        </g>
    }

    @* Whatever is still lying on the board to be picked up. A spare wall is drawn as
       what it gives you; a free move as an arrow going round again. *@
    @{ int laid = 0; List<(int Cell, bool IsWall)> lying = Pickups().ToList(); }
    @foreach ((int cell, bool isWall) in lying)
    {
        int cx = Centre(Board.ColOf(cell));
        int cy = Centre(Board.RowOf(cell));

        @* Positioned by the outer group so the inner one is free to be scaled about its
           own origin — everything inside is drawn around (0,0). Keyed on the game so a
           new board lays them out again; they follow the gaps rather than joining them,
           since a prize is worth reading after the hazard.

           The entrance is on the inner group, and the delay rides down to it as a custom
           property the way the gaps' and the mouths' do: animation-delay does not inherit
           and a custom property does. The holder cannot carry the entrance itself, because
           a keyframe's transform replaces this translate rather than composing with it —
           the prize would grow from the SVG origin, which on a 5x5 is off the board. *@
        <g @key="(Generation, cell)" class="pickup-holder"
           style="--delay: @(Stagger(laid++, lying.Count, 60, 110))ms"
           transform="translate(@cx @cy)">
            @if (isWall)
            {
                @* The same three faces a played wall stands on, in neutral ink and at the
                   same Lift — a spare wall lying loose on the square, which is exactly
                   what it is. Nothing about it has to be explained twice. *@
                <g class="pickup wall">
                    <rect class="cast" rx="2" x="@(-13 + Lift * Lamp)" y="@(-4 + (Lift + 1) * Lamp)" width="26" height="8" />
                    <rect class="side" rx="2" x="-13" y="-4" width="26" height="8" />
                    <rect class="top" rx="2" x="@(-13 - Lift * Lamp)" y="@(-4 - Lift * Lamp)" width="26" height="8" />
                </g>
            }
            else
            {
                @* A turn coming round again: three quarters of a circle with an arrowhead
                   at the end of it. It says "go again" without a word on it — now stamped
                   on a token sitting on the square rather than drawn onto the square. *@
                <g class="pickup skip">
                    <ellipse class="contact" cx="@(3 * Lamp)" cy="@(5 * Lamp)" rx="12" ry="5" />
                    <circle class="token" r="13" />
                    <circle class="dome" r="13" />
                    <path class="arc" d="M 6.36 -6.36 A 9 9 0 1 1 -6.36 -6.36" />
                    <path class="head" d="M -2.83 -9.90 L -4.10 -4.10 L -8.63 -8.63 Z" />
                </g>
            }
        </g>
    }

    @* The one taken on the last move, still drawn for a moment while it leaves. Keyed
       on the ply so each collection plays its own burst rather than reviving the last
       one; it is removed from the render as soon as the next move arrives.

       As with the prizes above, the group carries the square and the ring inside it does
       the opening: an animated transform replaces this one instead of composing with it,
       and a ring that opened at the SVG origin was off the view entirely on a 7x7. *@
    @if (Collected is { } taken)
    {
        <g @key="@($"taken-{State.Ply}")" class="pickup-burst @(taken.IsWall ? "wall" : "skip")"
           transform="translate(@Centre(Board.ColOf(taken.Cell)) @Centre(Board.RowOf(taken.Cell)))">
            <circle class="halo" r="15" />
        </g>
    }

    @* The lamp, laid over the furniture and nothing else. Everything above this line is
       something the board is made of — the tray, the tiles, the walls, the pickups — and
       everything below it is something the board is telling you, which has to be exactly
       as loud in the far corner as in the near one. Hence the order: a hint dot in the
       shaded corner would otherwise be dimmer than the same dot in the lit one.

       It sits inside the flip group, so the group's rotation is cancelled here rather
       than the light turning upside down with the board. 364 is the centre of the view
       on every board size, since a smaller game is a centred square of the same grid. *@
    <rect class="board-light" x="@ViewX" y="@ViewY" width="@ViewSize" height="@ViewSize" rx="6"
          transform="@(Flipped ? "rotate(180 364 364)" : "")" />

    @if (Reading)
    {
        @* The Front: the line where the two reaches meet. It is not a game object and
           neither player owns it, which is why it is drawn in an ink of its own — it is
           the shape the pieces make rather than a piece. Two passes of the same path, a
           broad haze under a thin seam, so it carries at a glance without becoming a
           border drawn on the board.

           Keyed on the path itself: when the turn passes, the squares both pawns reach in
           the same number of steps change hands, the seam moves a square, and it should
           arrive where it now is rather than slide there from where it was. *@
        @if (_front.Length > 0)
        {
            <g @key="_front" class="front">
                <path class="haze" d="@_front" />
                <path class="seam" d="@_front" />
            </g>
        }

        @* Ribbons rather than the dashed hairline they replace: a route is a way home and
           a body under a spine reads as one, where a dotted line reads as an overlay
           somebody left switched on. *@
        @foreach ((string accent, string points) in Routes())
        {
            <polyline class="ribbon body @accent" points="@points" />
            <polyline class="ribbon spine @accent" points="@points" />
        }
    }

    @* And what the groove under the pointer would actually do. The panel already says what
       it costs; this is the same sentence drawn on the board — the stretch of route that
       dies goes faint and dashed, and the way round draws itself in. Independent of the
       reading, because it answers a question you asked with the mouse. *@
    @if (Detour() is { } detour)
    {
        @if (detour.Gone.Length > 0)
        {
            <polyline class="detour gone @detour.Accent" points="@detour.Gone" />
        }

        @* Keyed on the groove, or crossing to the next one would update the path in place
           and the draw — which is the whole of how this arrives — would never play again. *@
        @if (detour.Now.Length > 0)
        {
            <polyline @key="_pointerWall" class="detour now @detour.Accent"
                      style="--len: @detour.Length" points="@detour.Now" />
        }
    }

    @if (LastMove is { } last)
    {
        <rect class="last @(LastMoverClass)" rx="3"
              x="@(LastX(last) - 3)" y="@(LastY(last) - 3)"
              width="@(LastWidth(last) + 6)" height="@(LastHeight(last) + 6)" />
    }

    @foreach (int cell in Hints())
    {
        <circle class="hint-dot @(State.SideToMove == 0 ? "p0" : "p1")"
                cx="@Centre(Board.ColOf(cell))" cy="@Centre(Board.RowOf(cell))" r="7" />
    }

    @* What the pointer is aiming at, what is waiting to be confirmed, and what is
       queued for when the turn arrives — the same three shapes, told apart by style. *@
    @foreach ((Move mark, string style) in Marks())
    {
        if (mark.Kind == MoveKind.Pawn)
        {
            <rect class="mark square @style" rx="3"
                  x="@Origin(mark.Col)" y="@Origin(mark.Row)" width="@Cell" height="@Cell" />
        }
        else
        {
            <rect class="mark bar @style" rx="2"
                  x="@WallX(mark.Kind, mark.Col)" y="@WallY(mark.Kind, mark.Row)"
                  width="@(mark.IsHorizontal ? WallLength : Gap)"
                  height="@(mark.IsHorizontal ? Gap : WallLength)" />
        }
    }

    @* Each pawn is drawn twice, once in each of two layers, and shown in exactly one of
       them. A jump has to pass over the piece it is jumping and an SVG has no z-index, so
       the piece that is moving must come later in the document than the one standing
       still — and reordering the two groups is not open to us: Blazor would move the
       element, and a moved element loses the transition the whole slide is made of.
       Two fixed copies with one of each pair never drawn buy an order that changes every
       move for four elements that are never painted. *@
    @for (int layer = 0; layer < 2; layer++)
    {
        @* The layer is a real group rather than only an order, so that each pawn is keyed
           against the other copy of itself and not against a square: keys are compared
           among siblings, and the squares above are already keyed on a triple. *@
        <g class="pawns">
            @for (int player = 0; player < 2; player++)
            {
                int cell = State.PawnOf(player);
                bool active = !State.IsGameOver && State.SideToMove == player;
                bool drawn = (_over == player) == (layer == 1);

                @* Placed by a transform on the group rather than by the circle's own
                   centre: that is what the slide animates, and moving a transform is far
                   cheaper for the browser than moving geometry.

                   Keyed on the board as well as the player: a pawn slides to where it
                   moved, but starting a different-sized game is not a move, and sliding
                   across the whole board for it would be nonsense. A new key means a new
                   element, which simply appears where it belongs. *@
                @* `warping` is the one move the holder must not carry: a portal's two
                   mouths are half a board apart, and the slide below would draw a diagonal
                   across ground the pawn never walked and walls it never passed. The class
                   arrives in the same render as the transform it cancels, which is what
                   lets the piece simply be at the far mouth while the group below plays
                   the leaving and the arriving. *@
                <g @key="(player, From)"
                   class="pawn-holder @(player == 0 ? "p0" : "p1") @(active ? "active" : "") @(drawn ? "" : "spare") @(_hop[player] == "warp" ? "warping" : "")"
                   transform="translate(@Centre(Board.ColOf(cell)) @Centre(Board.RowOf(cell)))">

                    @* The rise, which is the arc's half of a jump — the holder above
                       carries the piece across and this carries it over. Keyed on the
                       count of moves this pawn has made so a new one replaces the group
                       and plays again; the holder itself must keep its identity, or there
                       is no slide left for the rise to be half of.

                       The same group plays the warp, which is the other half of a portal
                       step: the offset back to the mouth it left is on the style, because
                       the piece is already at the far one by the time anything is drawn. *@
                    <g @key="(player, _hopAt[player])" class="lift @(_hop[player])" style="@WarpFrom(player)">

                        @* Contact first, because it is what the piece is standing on. It
                           spreads while the piece travels and tightens as it lands. It lies
                           away from the light, so it carries Lamp like the walls above. *@
                        <ellipse class="contact" cx="@(3 * Lamp)" cy="@(6 * Lamp)" rx="19" ry="7" />

                        @* Whose turn it is should be legible from the board, not only from
                           the panel beside it. A ring is a clearer answer than a
                           difference in opacity you have to compare two pieces to notice.

                           Keyed on the ply so the ring that is now on is a new element and
                           lands rather than simply being there. The pawn that just moved
                           gets a fresh group anyway; the one waiting for the turn does not,
                           and it is the one the turn is arriving at. *@
                        <circle class="ring" @key="(player, State.Ply)" r="26" />
                        <circle class="disc" r="20" />
                        <circle class="dome" r="20" />
                    </g>
                </g>
            }
        </g>
    }

    @* Hit targets last so they sit above everything they select. Squares first,
       grooves after, so a groove wins the pixels where they overlap — and how many that
       is depends on what is pointing at them, which is what Bleed answers. Like the
       squares, they stop at the edge of the game: a groove outside it would offer a
       wall nobody could ever be stopped by. *@
    @for (int row = From; row <= Last; row++)
    {
        for (int col = From; col <= Last; col++)
        {
            int r = row;
            int c = col;

            <rect class="hit" x="@Origin(col)" y="@Origin(row)" width="@Cell" height="@Cell"
                  @onpointerover="e => Over(e, MoveKind.Pawn, r, c)" />
        }
    }

    @for (int row = From; row < Last; row++)
    {
        for (int col = From; col <= Last; col++)
        {
            int r = row;
            int c = Math.Min(col, Last - 1);

            <rect class="hit" x="@Origin(col)" y="@(Origin(row) + Cell - Bleed)" width="@Cell" height="@GrooveReach"
                  @onpointerover="e => Over(e, MoveKind.HorizontalWall, r, c)" />
        }
    }

    @for (int row = From; row <= Last; row++)
    {
        for (int col = From; col < Last; col++)
        {
            int r = Math.Min(row, Last - 1);
            int c = col;

            <rect class="hit" x="@(Origin(col) + Cell - Bleed)" y="@Origin(row)" width="@GrooveReach" height="@Cell"
                  @onpointerover="e => Over(e, MoveKind.VerticalWall, r, c)" />
        }
    }

    @* The cursor: the one thing on this board a keyboard can reach, laid exactly over the
       hit target it is standing on so it blocks nothing the mouse was aiming at. Two
       hundred tab stops would be a board nobody could tab past; one that moves is the
       same board with a caret in it, and its label is where the caret is.

       Its key changes whenever a key moves it, which replaces the element rather than
       renaming it. A reader is told the name of what it has just been given and not
       reliably told that the name of what it already had has changed — so the cursor is
       handed over anew each time it moves, and focus is put back on it after the render.
       The pointer deliberately does not change the key: a press is a pointerover followed
       by a pointerdown, and an element replaced in between is a press that never lands.

       Drawn while the game is being read back as well as while it is being played. It is
       the only thing here that can hold focus, and a cursor that went out when the arrows
       stepped into the game so far would take the keyboard out of the page with it —
       where the labels are exactly as much use, since they name the position being read. *@
    <rect @key="_cursorKey" @ref="_cursor" class="hit cursor" tabindex="0" role="button"
          aria-label="@AimLabel" aria-disabled="@AimBlocked"
          x="@AimX" y="@AimY" width="@AimWidth" height="@AimHeight"
          @onpointerover="e => Over(e, _aimKind, _aimRow, _aimCol)" @onclick="Activate" />

    </g>
</svg>

@* What just happened, for somebody who cannot see it happen. The board is drawn in
   shapes and a reader is given none of them, so the one sentence that matters — the
   move that arrived and whose turn it now is — is put where a reader is listening.

   Hidden here rather than in the stylesheet on purpose. This element exists only for a
   screen reader, and a stylesheet that has not been told about it would leave a sentence
   printed beside the board: the one kind of mistake this whole round is against. It is
   out of flow, so it changes no layout it lands in.

   Only the position feeds it. A hover redraws the board a dozen times a second and a
   live region that repeated itself each time would be a voice nobody could play through;
   the same text is not spoken twice. *@
<div class="board-say" role="status" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true"
     style="position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;margin:-1px;overflow:hidden;clip-path:inset(50%);white-space:nowrap">@Announcement</div>

@code {
    private const int Pad = 28;
    private const int Cell = 64;
    private const int Gap = 12;
    private const int Pitch = Cell + Gap;
    private const int Extent = Pad * 2 + Board.Size * Cell + (Board.Size - 1) * Gap;
    private const int WallLength = Cell * 2 + Gap;

    /// <summary>
    /// How far the field is sunk inside the rim. Narrower than <see cref="Pad"/> on
    /// purpose: the coordinate labels are drawn at 14 and 12 units in from the edge, so
    /// they land on the rim band and read as printed on the frame.
    /// </summary>
    private const int Frame = 22;

    /// <summary>
    /// How far a placed wall stands proud of its groove, in the board's own units so it
    /// scales with the board and is the same piece on a 5x5. Mirrored by --lift in the
    /// stylesheet, which the drop entrance is written against.
    /// </summary>
    private const int Lift = 4;

    /// <summary>
    /// How far inside the mouth of a gap its floor sits. Wide enough that the wall of the
    /// opening is a band you can read the light across rather than a hairline.
    /// </summary>
    private const int PitDepth = 7;

    /// <summary>
    /// How far ahead in the race a square has to be before its tint stops deepening. Past
    /// six steps the answer is simply "theirs" and a darker square says nothing more; below
    /// it, the depth of the stain is the size of the lead.
    /// </summary>
    private const int Decisive = 6;

    /// <summary>
    /// How far a square is restained toward its owner, in percent, at a dead heat and at a
    /// decisive lead. The floor is not zero on purpose: a square that is contested is still
    /// somebody's next step, and the seam is drawn by the line between the two colours
    /// rather than by a band of bare board between them.
    /// </summary>
    private const int FaintestStain = 10;

    private const int StrongestStain = 44;

    /// <summary>First row and column in the game — non-zero only on a smaller board.</summary>
    private int From => State.GoalRow(0);

    /// <summary>Last row and column in the game.</summary>
    private int Last => State.GoalRow(1);

    private int Span => Last - From + 1;

    private int ViewX => From * Pitch;

    private int ViewY => ViewX;

    private int ViewSize => (Span - 1) * Pitch + Cell + Pad * 2;

    private string ViewBox => $"{ViewX} {ViewY} {ViewSize} {ViewSize}";

    /// <summary>
    /// The half turn a gradient needs to survive the board being turned around, or null
    /// when it is not. An object-bound gradient is painted in its own shape's box and then
    /// carried through every transform above it, so the rotation that puts the second seat
    /// at the near edge takes the light with it: tiles bevelled along the bottom, openings
    /// with their lit wall on the side away from the lamp. Pre-rotating the paint by the
    /// same half turn cancels the group's, so one lamp stays above and to the left for both
    /// seats. The lamp gradient itself is in user space and cancels the rotation on the
    /// element instead; the round ones are symmetrical about their centre and need neither.
    /// </summary>
    private string? TurnedPaint => Flipped ? "rotate(180 0.5 0.5)" : null;

    /// <summary>
    /// The sign every offset that says where the lamp is has to be drawn with. The gradients
    /// above cancel the group's half turn in the paint; the cues that are geometry rather
    /// than paint — the face of a wall lifted toward the lamp, the shadow it throws away
    /// from it, the ellipse a piece stands on — had no such cancellation and turned with the
    /// board. Measured on screen in board units at the second seat, the lit top face sat 4
    /// down and 4 right of the bar it belongs to and the shadow 4 up and 5 left of it: a
    /// wall lit from underneath, throwing its shadow up into the light — every cue exactly
    /// inverted from the first seat. Negating the offset in board space puts it back
    /// where the screen wants it, because a half turn about the board's centre is exactly a
    /// negation of every offset inside it.
    ///
    /// None of it reaches the flat board. Every shape this signs — a wall's `cast` and
    /// `top`, a spare wall's, and the `contact` under a piece and under a token — is
    /// `fill: none` in the stylesheet until `carved` is on, so what moves here is a handful
    /// of rects and ellipses that board never paints.
    /// </summary>
    private int Lamp => Flipped ? -1 : 1;

    // What the pointer is over, recorded whether or not it is our turn. Dropping it
    // when the turn passes is what made a resting cursor go dead: you would have to
    // move off the square and back before the click would take.
    private int _pointerCell = -1;
    private Move? _pointerWall;

    // Where the keyboard cursor is standing, which is a target and not a move: a square
    // when the kind is Pawn, and the wall slot under it otherwise. It outlives the aim
    // above — the mouse may leave the board, and the cursor is still where it was left.
    private MoveKind _aimKind = MoveKind.Pawn;
    private int _aimRow = -1;
    private int _aimCol = -1;

    // Which of the two is aiming. Both write the same aim, so the board downstream never
    // asks where a target came from; this is only here so that a mouse resting on the
    // board cannot quietly take the cursor back off a player who is using the keys.
    private bool _byKeys;

    // Where the pointer was the last time it reported. A browser dispatches pointerover
    // when an element moves under a still cursor as well as when the cursor moves onto
    // one, and the first of those is not the pointer asking for anything.
    private double _pointerX = double.NaN;
    private double _pointerY = double.NaN;

    // Whether a press that began on this board is still in progress. It is the whole of
    // what a finger has instead of a hover: while it is set the player is looking at a
    // move without having played it, and the release is what plays it.
    private bool _pressed;

    // When the last press was released, so that the click a browser raises afterwards can
    // be told from one an assistive technology raised on its own. See Activate.
    private long _releasedAt = -GhostClickMs;

    // Set when a move is waiting to be confirmed. On a phone there is no hover, so a
    // first tap should choose and a second commit, rather than one stray tap moving.
    private Move? _selected;

    private bool _needsRender = true;

    // Where each pawn stood the last time a position was handed in. The board is told
    // where the pieces are and never what happened, so this is the only place the
    // difference between a step and a jump can be recovered from.
    private readonly int[] _wasAt = { -1, -1 };

    // Counted rather than flagged: the key the rise hangs on has to be a value the
    // browser has not seen before, or the group is reused and the arc never replays.
    private readonly int[] _hopAt = { 0, 0 };

    private readonly string[] _hop = { "", "" };

    // Where the pawn left from. Every other move is drawn where the piece now is and needs
    // nothing, but a portal step is played out at the mouth it leaves before it is played
    // at the mouth it arrives at, and by then the board has only been told the second one.
    private readonly int[] _hopFrom = { -1, -1 };

    // Which pawn is drawn in the front layer. The one that moved last, so that a jump
    // passes over the piece it is jumping rather than behind it.
    private int _over = 1;

    // What the reading says about the position: who reaches each square first, by how
    // much, and the path along which the two answers meet. Held rather than recomputed
    // per element, because eighty-one squares ask the same two flood fills; filled only
    // while the reading is up, so an ordinary board pays nothing at all for it.
    private readonly int[] _owner = new int[Board.CellCount];
    private readonly int[] _stain = new int[Board.CellCount];
    private string _front = string.Empty;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public GameState State { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public bool Interactive { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Flatten the board into a chart of the race: the squares stained toward whoever
    /// reaches them first, the seam between the two reaches, and both routes home. Held
    /// up deliberately and put down again — none of it is visible when it is off.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Reading { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// This position is being looked at rather than played. Everything the board does to
    /// announce a change — a wall landing, a gap opening, a prize being laid out, a pawn
    /// going over another — is switched off, and the pawns are left to glide between the
    /// positions the arrow keys ask for. Stepping back through a game is reading it, and
    /// a board that replays its own entrance forty times is one you are fighting.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Reviewing { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Turn the board around, for the player who took the second seat.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Flipped { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Draw the board as a made object rather than as a diagram: lit, bevelled, with the
    /// walls standing out of their grooves. Off is the board this game is — flat, quiet
    /// and high in contrast — and everything the material adds is scoped under `.carved`
    /// in the stylesheet, so nothing of it survives the switch being off.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Carved { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Squares out of play, drawn as gaps rather than squares.</summary>
    [Parameter] public UInt128 Holes { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Counts the games, so a new one can be told from a move within one. A rolled board
    /// is a different board every time and the things that make it different — the gaps
    /// and the prizes — are worth a moment to read before the clock starts. Changing this
    /// rebuilds the board's elements, which is what lets their entrance play again.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public int Generation { get; set; }

    private int HoleCount => (int)UInt128.PopCount(Holes);

    /// <summary>
    /// Where the nth of <paramref name="count"/> things falls inside a fixed window,
    /// rather than a step each. The window has to stay put however many there are: this
    /// build has one thread and the search takes it as soon as the pause after a move
    /// runs out, so an entrance still going when that happens stops dead — and stopped
    /// at the start of one of these is stopped at nothing visible. Ten pickups and two
    /// gaps therefore arrive in the same time two pickups do, just closer together.
    /// </summary>
    private static int Stagger(int index, int count, int start, int window) =>
        count <= 1 ? start : start + window * index / (count - 1);

    /// <summary>The pickup taken by the last move, if it took one, for its send-off.</summary>
    [Parameter] public (int Cell, bool IsWall)? Collected { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Require a second tap on the same target before the move is played.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool ConfirmMoves { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the thing pointing at this board is a finger rather than a mouse. It changes
    /// one number — how far a groove reaches into the squares beside it — and nothing else,
    /// because everything else about the interaction is already the same for both.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Coarse { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// How far a groove's hit target reaches into the square on either side of it, in the
    /// board's own units. A mouse lands where it is pointed and needs almost none: four
    /// units either side of a twelve-unit groove is twenty of the seventy-six a square and
    /// its groove share, and the other fifty-six belong to the square.
    ///
    /// A finger does not land where it is pointed. On a phone-sized board a square is about
    /// forty screen pixels across, so twenty units is five pixels of groove — a target
    /// nobody can hit, and walls are half the game. Twelve either side splits the pitch
    /// roughly in half, which is where the split belongs when the pointer is a blunt one:
    /// the nearest feature wins, and the nearest feature to the top of a square is the
    /// square while the nearest to its edge is the groove.
    ///
    /// The board does not have to be right first time for this to work. The press aims and
    /// the release plays, so a finger that lands one groove out slides onto the right one
    /// before it lifts.
    /// </summary>
    private int Bleed => Coarse ? 12 : 4;

    /// <summary>How thick a groove's hit target is, across the groove.</summary>
    private int GrooveReach => Gap + 2 * Bleed;

    [Parameter] public Move? LastMove { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public EventCallback<Move> OnMove { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether a press on a board that is not taking moves should be banked for the moment
    /// the turn arrives. It is not the negation of <see cref="Interactive"/>, which folds two
    /// unrelated reasons together: it is not your turn, and you are reading the game back.
    /// Only the first of those is a reason to queue anything — a press on a position that is
    /// forty plies old is a player reading, and a move banked there is played later without
    /// ever having been asked for. Whoever owns the game says which of the two it is; the
    /// board cannot tell them apart from <see cref="Interactive"/> alone.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool AllowPremove { get; set; }

    /// <summary>A move queued for the moment the turn arrives, or null for none.</summary>
    [Parameter] public Move? Premove { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public EventCallback<Move?> OnPremove { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Reports what the wall under the cursor would cost, or null when there is none.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<string?> OnPreview { get; set; }

    protected override void OnParametersSet()
    {
        ReadMotion();

        // The reading is a property of the position and of nothing else, so it is worked
        // out when a position arrives rather than when the pointer moves — a hover does
        // not come through here, and there are eighty-one squares asking.
        if (Reading) ReadPosition();

        // The cursor is not moved by a move either, for the same reason — but a game of
        // another size is another board, and a cursor left standing outside it would be
        // a caret nobody could see.
        PlaceAim();

        // The pointer is deliberately not cleared here. What it is over has not changed
        // just because the position did; only what that means has.
        _needsRender = true;
        _selected = null;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Puts the focus back on the cursor after a key has moved it. The element is a new
    /// one each time — that is what has the reader name it — so the focus it had goes
    /// with the old one and is handed to the new one here.
    /// </summary>
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (!_focusWanted) return;
        _focusWanted = false;

        try
        {
            // Without this the browser scrolls the board into view on every press, and
            // on a phone that is the page jumping under a player who only moved a caret.
            await _cursor.FocusAsync(preventScroll: true);
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            // The cursor went with a render — a game ended, a review started. The next
            // press asks again, and a board that cannot focus is not a board that breaks.
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Works out how each pawn got where it now is, by comparing it with where it was the
    /// last time a position arrived. Nothing else on the board can tell a jump from a
    /// step, and until this existed a pawn jumping the other one slid straight through it.
    /// Runs on every parameter set, including the ones a hover causes, so it has to be
    /// silent when nothing has actually moved.
    /// </summary>
    private void ReadMotion()
    {
        for (int player = 0; player < 2; player++)
        {
            int now = State.PawnOf(player);
            int was = _wasAt[player];

            _wasAt[player] = now;
            if (was == now) continue;

            _hopFrom[player] = was;

            // A first position, a new game, or a step back through the move list is not
            // travel. There is nothing to go over, and the pawn simply is where it is —
            // the slide still carries it there, because that is what the arrows are for.
            //
            // A portal step is the exception to the exception. Reading a game back is still
            // no reason to glide a pawn diagonally across half a board it never walked, so
            // the one move that is not travel is drawn as what it is at every speed.
            _hop[player] = was < 0 ? ""
                : IsPortalStep(was, now) ? "warp"
                : Reviewing ? ""
                : Motion(was, now);

            _hopAt[player]++;
            _over = player;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the pawn travelled a portal rather than the board. Asked of the position and
    /// not of the distance: the pairing is the half turn that maps one player's half onto
    /// the other's, so the two mouths are simply two squares, and nothing about where they
    /// are says that stepping between them was legal — only the mask does.
    /// </summary>
    private bool IsPortalStep(int from, int to) =>
        State.HasPortals && GameState.PortalPartner(from) == to && State.IsPortalMouth(to);

    /// <summary>
    /// The offset back to the mouth a portal step left from, in the board's own units, for
    /// the half of the animation that has to play where the piece actually was. Null for
    /// every other move, all of which are drawn where the piece now is.
    /// </summary>
    private string? WarpFrom(int player)
    {
        if (_hop[player] != "warp") return null;

        int from = _hopFrom[player];
        int to = State.PawnOf(player);

        return $"--from-x: {Centre(Board.ColOf(from)) - Centre(Board.ColOf(to))}px;" +
               $" --from-y: {Centre(Board.RowOf(from)) - Centre(Board.RowOf(to))}px";
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// One square is a step; a corner is the diagonal a jump falls back on when the square
    /// behind the other pawn is blocked; two squares in a line is a jump over it, which is
    /// the one that has to visibly go over rather than through. Anything further apart is
    /// not a move at all and gets nothing.
    /// </summary>
    private static string Motion(int from, int to)
    {
        int rows = Math.Abs(Board.RowOf(to) - Board.RowOf(from));
        int cols = Math.Abs(Board.ColOf(to) - Board.ColOf(from));

        return (rows, cols) switch
        {
            (0, 1) or (1, 0) => "step",
            (1, 1) => "slip",
            (0, 2) or (2, 0) => "leap",
            _ => "",
        };
    }

    // Mouse movement inside one target must not redraw three hundred elements.
    protected override bool ShouldRender()
    {
        bool render = _needsRender;
        _needsRender = false;
        return render;
    }

    private string LastMoverClass => State.SideToMove == 0 ? "p1" : "p0";

    private RenderFragment Coordinates => builder =>
    {
        int sequence = 0;

        // Only the squares in the game are labelled, against the edges of what is shown
        // rather than of the whole grid, and counted from the game's own corner — a 7x7
        // board runs a1 to g7, which is also what the move list calls those squares.
        for (int i = From; i <= Last; i++)
        {
            // The labels are not rotated with the board, so they are renumbered instead.
            int row = Flipped ? Quoridor.Core.Board.Size - 1 - i : i;

            Label($"{Last - row + 1}", ViewX + 14, Centre(i) + 4);
            Label($"{(char)('a' + row - From)}", Centre(i), ViewY + ViewSize - Pad + 16);
        }

        void Label(string text, int x, int y)
        {
            builder.OpenElement(sequence++, "text");
            builder.AddAttribute(sequence++, "class", "coord");
            builder.AddAttribute(sequence++, "x", x);
            builder.AddAttribute(sequence++, "y", y);
            builder.AddContent(sequence++, text);
            builder.CloseElement();
        }
    };

    // =============================================================== interaction ==

    /// <summary>The move the pointer is currently over, whoever's turn it is.</summary>
    private Move? Pointed => _pointerWall ?? (_pointerCell >= 0 ? Move.ToCell(_pointerCell) : null);

    /// <summary>Legal for the side to move right now. Meaningless during their turn.</summary>
    private bool PointedIsLegal => Pointed is { } move && State.IsLegal(move);

    /// <summary>
    /// Takes aim at a target, whichever of the two asked for it. The cursor and the pointer
    /// write the same aim on purpose: everything below — the mark, the sentence in the
    /// panel, the detour a groove would cause — is drawn from this and never asks which of
    /// them put it here.
    /// </summary>
    private void AimAt(MoveKind kind, int row, int col)
    {
        _aimKind = kind;
        _aimRow = row;
        _aimCol = col;

        if (kind == MoveKind.Pawn)
        {
            int cell = Board.Index(row, col);
            if (_pointerCell == cell && _pointerWall is null) return;

            _pointerCell = cell;
            _pointerWall = null;
            _needsRender = true;

            OnPreview.InvokeAsync(null);
            return;
        }

        var candidate = new Move(kind, row, col);
        if (_pointerWall == candidate) return;

        _pointerWall = candidate;
        _pointerCell = -1;
        _needsRender = true;

        OnPreview.InvokeAsync(Interactive ? Describe(candidate, State.IsWallLegal(kind, row, col)) : null);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The pointer is over a target: a mouse hovering it, or a finger pressed on it and
    /// sliding across it. The two are the same event and mean the same thing here — this
    /// is where the aim is, and nothing is played until the pointer is lifted off.
    /// </summary>
    private void Over(PointerEventArgs e, MoveKind kind, int row, int col)
    {
        if (PointerAsked(e)) AimAt(kind, row, col);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the pointer is actually asking for the target it has just reported, and with
    /// it hands the aim back from the keys. A browser raises pointerover when an element
    /// arrives under a still pointer as well as when the pointer arrives on an element,
    /// and the cursor moving on the keys does exactly that — so a hand resting anywhere on
    /// the board would pull every press straight back to where the hand was. The pointer's
    /// own coordinates are the only thing that can tell the two apart.
    ///
    /// Only a pointer that can hover is asked. A finger cannot be anywhere without being
    /// pressed there, so every report from one is a contact that has just been made and is
    /// unambiguously somebody asking for something — including the second tap on the square
    /// the first one was on, which arrives at coordinates identical to the last and which
    /// the arithmetic here would otherwise read as a caret sliding out from underneath a
    /// hand that is not there.
    /// </summary>
    private bool PointerAsked(PointerEventArgs e)
    {
        bool moved = e.ClientX != _pointerX || e.ClientY != _pointerY;

        _pointerX = e.ClientX;
        _pointerY = e.ClientY;

        if (_byKeys && !moved && e.PointerType is "mouse" or "pen") return false;

        PointerTakesOver();
        return true;
    }

    /// <summary>The pointer has the aim now, and the caret on the board is no longer what
    /// the player is steering by.</summary>
    private void PointerTakesOver()
    {
        if (!_byKeys) return;

        _byKeys = false;
        _needsRender = true;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The pointer has gone down on the board. Nothing is played here: what a press does is
    /// aim, and a finger that has just landed on the wrong groove has until it lifts to
    /// slide onto the right one. The aim itself is already correct — the pointerover that
    /// precedes every press set it — so this only records that a press is in progress and
    /// asks for a repaint, which is what puts the wall and its cost on the screen under a
    /// thumb that has not committed to anything yet.
    /// </summary>
    private void Press(PointerEventArgs e)
    {
        // The primary button only. A right or middle press is the browser's business, and
        // a context menu that also placed a wall would be a wall nobody asked for.
        if (e.Button != 0) return;

        _pressed = true;
        PointerTakesOver();
        _needsRender = true;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Lifting plays what is aimed at. Only after a press that started on this board: a
    /// release that arrives having begun somewhere else — a button in the panel, a drag
    /// from outside the page — is not a move being asked for.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task Release(PointerEventArgs e)
    {
        if (e.Button != 0) return;
        if (!_pressed) return;

        _pressed = false;
        _releasedAt = Environment.TickCount64;

        await Commit();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The cursor being activated as the button it says it is. A physical keyboard never
    /// arrives here — Enter and Space are answered as keys, above — and neither does a
    /// pointer, which is answered by the press and the release. What is left is an
    /// assistive technology activating the element on the player's behalf, which is a
    /// synthetic click and nothing else, and without this it would be the one way of
    /// playing a move that this board does not answer.
    ///
    /// A pointer does raise a click as well, after the release that has already played the
    /// move — so a click that follows one is dropped. By the clock rather than by a flag: a
    /// release that produced no click at all would leave a flag standing, and the next
    /// genuine activation would be the one it swallowed.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task Activate()
    {
        if (Environment.TickCount64 - _releasedAt < GhostClickMs) return;

        await Commit();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// How long after a release a click is still that release's own. Generous: the cost of
    /// being wrong one way is a move played twice, and the other way is an activation
    /// ignored by somebody who can simply do it again.
    /// </summary>
    private const int GhostClickMs = 700;

    /// <summary>
    /// The press has been taken away rather than finished — the system claiming the gesture
    /// as a scroll or a back swipe, a call arriving. Nothing is played, and the aim goes
    /// with it, because there is no pointer left on the board to own one.
    /// </summary>
    private void Abandon()
    {
        _pressed = false;
        ClearHover();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The pointer has left the board. That ends a press as well as clearing the aim, which
    /// is what makes sliding off the board and lifting there the way to take a press back —
    /// the one cancelling gesture a finger has, since it cannot simply stop hovering.
    /// </summary>
    private void LeaveBoard()
    {
        _pressed = false;
        ClearHover();
    }

    private void ClearHover()
    {
        // Leaving the board takes the pointer's aim with it and leaves the keys' alone.
        // A cursor that vanished because a hand brushed past the board on its way
        // somewhere else would be a cursor nobody could rely on.
        if (_byKeys) return;
        if (_pointerWall is null && _pointerCell < 0) return;

        _pointerWall = null;
        _pointerCell = -1;
        _needsRender = true;

        OnPreview.InvokeAsync(null);
    }

    private async Task Commit()
    {
        if (Pointed is not { } move) return;

        // Not our turn: remember it and play it the moment the turn arrives. Whether it
        // will still be legal is unknowable now, so it is checked then and dropped if
        // the opponent's move ruined it.
        //
        // Asked of AllowPremove and not of !Interactive, which is also false while the game
        // is being read back — and a press on a forty-ply-old position is a player reading,
        // not a player queueing. Banked there it survived the return to the live board and
        // was played after the engine's next reply, a move nobody had asked for.
        if (!Interactive)
        {
            if (!AllowPremove) return;

            if (Premove is null || Premove != move) await OnPremove.InvokeAsync(move);
            else await OnPremove.InvokeAsync(null);

            return;
        }

        if (!State.IsLegal(move)) return;

        // With confirmation on, the first press chooses and the second plays. It is no
        // longer what a touch screen needs — holding the press is the look now, and lifting
        // is the commit — but it is still what somebody who wants a move to take two
        // deliberate acts asked for, on either kind of pointer.
        if (ConfirmMoves && _selected != move)
        {
            _selected = move;
            _needsRender = true;
            return;
        }

        _selected = null;
        await OnMove.InvokeAsync(move);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// What the wall would actually do. Checking legality already walks both routes,
    /// so the number that decides whether it is worth playing is nearly free.
    /// </summary>
    private string Describe(Move wall, bool legal)
    {
        if (!legal)
        {
            return State.IsSlotFree(wall.Kind, wall.Row, wall.Col)
                ? "That would leave a player with no route at all — not allowed."
                : "No room for a wall there.";
        }

        int mover = State.SideToMove;
        int opponent = mover ^ 1;

        GameState placed = State;
        placed.PlaceWallUnchecked(wall.Kind, wall.Row, wall.Col);

        int them = PathFinder.Distance(placed, opponent) - PathFinder.Distance(State, opponent);
        int us = PathFinder.Distance(placed, mover) - PathFinder.Distance(State, mover);

        string cost = them switch
        {
            0 => "Costs them nothing",
            1 => "Costs them 1 step",
            _ => $"Costs them {them} steps",
        };

        return us == 0 ? $"{cost}, and nothing to you." : $"{cost}, and {us} to you.";
    }

    // =================================================================== keyboard ==

    /// <summary>The cursor element, so the focus can be put back on it once it has moved.</summary>
    private ElementReference _cursor;

    /// <summary>
    /// Counts the moves of the cursor. It is the cursor element's key, so every press
    /// hands the browser a new element to name rather than a renamed one — see the cursor
    /// in the markup for why that difference is the whole of whether a reader hears it.
    /// </summary>
    private int _cursorKey;

    private bool _focusWanted;

    /// <summary>
    /// The keys, which are the whole of what it takes to play a game here without a mouse.
    /// Arrows move a caret in the directions of the screen, Enter or Space plays what it
    /// is standing on, W or R takes it into the grooves and turns the wall there, Escape
    /// brings it back out, and Home returns it to your own piece.
    ///
    /// The presses arrive from the cursor itself — it is the only thing on the board that
    /// can hold focus — so nothing here fires unless the board is what the player is on.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task OnKeyDown(KeyboardEventArgs e)
    {
        // Ctrl+Z is the page's undo and the rest of the modifiers are the browser's own.
        // Nothing the board wants is worth taking one of them away.
        if (e.CtrlKey || e.MetaKey || e.AltKey) return;

        switch (e.Key)
        {
            // Screen directions and not board ones. The board is turned a half turn for
            // the second seat, and a caret that turned with it would go left when it was
            // asked to go right — which is worse than having no caret at all.
            case "ArrowUp": Step(-1, 0); break;
            case "ArrowDown": Step(1, 0); break;
            case "ArrowLeft": Step(0, -1); break;
            case "ArrowRight": Step(0, 1); break;

            // Into the grooves, and once there, round. Which slot and which way it lies
            // are the same question asked twice, so they are the same key asked twice.
            case "w" or "W" or "r" or "R":
                TurnWall();
                break;

            // Back to your own piece, from wherever forty presses have left the caret.
            case "Home":
                RestAim();
                break;

            case "Escape":
                if (!LeaveWalls()) return;
                break;

            case "Enter" or " ":
                AimByKeys();
                await Commit();

                // With confirmation on, the first press only chooses, and a press that
                // says nothing back is one a reader cannot tell from a press that failed.
                // What is waiting is in the cursor's label, so the cursor is handed over
                // again to have that label read out.
                if (_selected is not null)
                {
                    _cursorKey++;
                    _focusWanted = true;
                }

                return;

            default:
                return;
        }

        AimByKeys();

        _cursorKey++;
        _focusWanted = true;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Moves the cursor a square, or a slot while it is in the grooves, and stops at the
    /// edge of the game rather than wrapping round it: a caret that comes out at the far
    /// side is a caret you have to go looking for.
    /// </summary>
    private void Step(int rows, int cols)
    {
        if (Flipped)
        {
            rows = -rows;
            cols = -cols;
        }

        int high = _aimKind == MoveKind.Pawn ? Last : Last - 1;

        _aimRow = Math.Clamp(_aimRow + rows, From, high);
        _aimCol = Math.Clamp(_aimCol + cols, From, high);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Takes the cursor into the grooves, and turns the wall once it is there. A square's
    /// own slot is the corner below and to the right of it, which is the wall a player
    /// looking at that square is thinking about; the far row and column have no corner of
    /// their own, and the cursor steps back onto the last one that has.
    /// </summary>
    private void TurnWall()
    {
        if (_aimKind != MoveKind.Pawn)
        {
            _aimKind = _aimKind == MoveKind.HorizontalWall ? MoveKind.VerticalWall : MoveKind.HorizontalWall;
            return;
        }

        _aimKind = MoveKind.HorizontalWall;
        _aimRow = Math.Min(_aimRow, Last - 1);
        _aimCol = Math.Min(_aimCol, Last - 1);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Escape, which puts back whatever the last press put you into: the grooves first,
    /// then a move standing waiting to be confirmed. False when the board has nothing to
    /// leave, and the key is then the page's — where it means "stop reading the game".
    /// </summary>
    private bool LeaveWalls()
    {
        if (_aimKind != MoveKind.Pawn)
        {
            _aimKind = MoveKind.Pawn;
            return true;
        }

        if (_selected is null) return false;

        _selected = null;
        return true;
    }

    /// <summary>Puts the cursor on the piece whose turn it is, which is where the next
    /// move is being thought about before it is aimed anywhere.</summary>
    private void RestAim()
    {
        int cell = State.PawnOf(State.SideToMove);

        _aimKind = MoveKind.Pawn;
        _aimRow = Board.RowOf(cell);
        _aimCol = Board.ColOf(cell);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Brings the cursor inside the game actually being played. A cursor stays where it
    /// was left for the whole of a game — a caret that jumped every time the position
    /// changed would be one you had to find again after every move — but a new game is
    /// not that: nothing on the board is where it was, and the caret goes home with it.
    /// A smaller board makes the point twice over, since a cursor left where a nine put
    /// it can be off the five entirely.
    /// </summary>
    private void PlaceAim()
    {
        int high = _aimKind == MoveKind.Pawn ? Last : Last - 1;
        bool inside = _aimRow >= From && _aimRow <= high && _aimCol >= From && _aimCol <= high;

        if (inside && Generation == _placedFor) return;

        _placedFor = Generation;
        RestAim();
    }

    /// <summary>Which game the cursor was last placed for. Not nullable and not zero:
    /// zero is a real generation, and a board is one of a great many.</summary>
    private int _placedFor = -1;

    /// <summary>Hands the aim to the cursor, wherever the last press left it.</summary>
    private void AimByKeys()
    {
        _byKeys = true;
        _needsRender = true;

        AimAt(_aimKind, _aimRow, _aimCol);
    }

    /// <summary>What the cursor is standing on, as the move playing it would be.</summary>
    private Move AimMove => new(_aimKind, _aimRow, _aimCol);

    // Laid exactly over the hit target underneath it, which is why it is written in Bleed
    // rather than in a number: the target is a different size under a finger, and a caret
    // drawn to the mouse's size there would take presses away from the targets on either
    // side of the one it is standing on.
    private int AimX => _aimKind == MoveKind.VerticalWall ? Origin(_aimCol) + Cell - Bleed : Origin(_aimCol);

    private int AimY => _aimKind == MoveKind.HorizontalWall ? Origin(_aimRow) + Cell - Bleed : Origin(_aimRow);

    private int AimWidth => _aimKind == MoveKind.VerticalWall ? GrooveReach : Cell;

    private int AimHeight => _aimKind == MoveKind.HorizontalWall ? GrooveReach : Cell;

    /// <summary>Said of a target that cannot be played from here, so a reader is told
    /// before pressing rather than by the press doing nothing.</summary>
    private string? AimBlocked => Interactive && !State.IsLegal(AimMove) ? "true" : null;

    /// <summary>
    /// Where the cursor is, in the notation printed round the board and used by the move
    /// list — so a player who cannot see the board is given the same names as one who can,
    /// and the two of them can talk about the same game.
    /// </summary>
    private string AimLabel
    {
        get
        {
            Move aim = AimMove;
            string spot = Notation.Format(aim, State);

            if (aim.Kind == MoveKind.Pawn)
            {
                string what = Occupant(aim.Cell);

                if (!Interactive || !State.IsLegal(aim)) return $"{spot}, {what}";

                return $"{spot}, {what}, {Offer("step here")}";
            }

            // The move list calls it e3h. A reader hears a letter as a letter, so the one
            // that says which way the wall lies is given as the word it stands for.
            string wall = $"wall {spot[..^1]} {(aim.IsHorizontal ? "across" : "down")}";

            if (!Interactive) return wall;

            return State.IsWallLegal(aim.Kind, aim.Row, aim.Col)
                ? $"{wall}, {Offer("place it here")}"
                : $"{wall}, not allowed";
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// What pressing here would do. Nearly always the plain offer — but with confirmation
    /// on, the target already chosen is one press from being played, and a board that said
    /// the same thing before and after the choosing would leave a reader unable to tell a
    /// press that chose from a press that did nothing at all.
    /// </summary>
    private string Offer(string plain) => _selected == AimMove ? "press again to play it" : plain;

    /// <summary>What is standing on a square, in the words the guides use for it.</summary>
    private string Occupant(int cell)
    {
        UInt128 bit = Board.Bit(cell);

        if ((Holes & bit) != 0) return "a gap";
        if (State.PawnOf(0) == cell) return "the first player";
        if (State.PawnOf(1) == cell) return "the second player";
        if ((State.WallPickups & bit) != 0) return "two spare walls";
        if ((State.SkipPickups & bit) != 0) return "a free move";
        if (State.IsPortalMouth(cell)) return "a portal";

        return "empty";
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The one sentence the board itself has to say out loud: what was just played, and
    /// who is on move now. Composed here rather than taken from the panel because the
    /// panel's status is about the game — thinking, waiting, disconnected — and this is
    /// about the position, which is the part a reader cannot get at any other way.
    /// </summary>
    private string Announcement
    {
        get
        {
            if (State.IsGameOver) return $"{Seat(State.Winner)} wins.";

            string turn = $"{Seat(State.SideToMove)} to move.";

            return LastMove is { } last ? $"{Spoken(last)} {turn}" : turn;
        }
    }

    private static string Seat(int player) => player == 0 ? "First player" : "Second player";

    private string Spoken(Move move) => move.Kind == MoveKind.Pawn
        ? $"Pawn to {Notation.Format(move, State)}."
        : $"Wall {Notation.Format(move, State)[..^1]} {(move.IsHorizontal ? "across" : "down")}.";

    // =================================================================== contents ==

    /// <summary>Every square still holding something, and whether it is a spare wall.</summary>
    private IEnumerable<(int Cell, bool IsWall)> Pickups()
    {
        UInt128 walls = State.WallPickups;
        UInt128 skips = State.SkipPickups;

        for (int cell = 0; cell < Board.CellCount && (walls | skips) != 0; cell++)
        {
            UInt128 bit = Board.Bit(cell);

            if ((walls & bit) != 0)
            {
                walls &= ~bit;
                yield return (cell, true);
            }
            else if ((skips & bit) != 0)
            {
                skips &= ~bit;
                yield return (cell, false);
            }
        }
    }

    private IEnumerable<(Move Move, string Style)> Marks()
    {
        string side = State.SideToMove == 0 ? "p0" : "p1";

        // A pointer is its own cursor and needs no mark to say where it is; a caret has
        // nothing else, so the target it stands on is drawn whether or not the target is
        // one that could be played from here.
        string caret = _byKeys ? " cursor" : string.Empty;

        if (Premove is { } queued) yield return (queued, "premove");

        if (_selected is { } chosen)
        {
            yield return (chosen, $"chosen {side}");

            // Confirming leaves the cursor free to go on looking, and a caret that went
            // out while a move waited would be a board the keys had gone quiet on.
            if (_byKeys && Pointed is { } looking && looking != chosen)
                yield return (looking, "cursor");

            yield break;
        }

        if (Interactive && Pointed is { } pointed)
            yield return (pointed, (PointedIsLegal ? $"aim {side}" : "bad") + caret);
        else if (_byKeys && Pointed is { } waiting)
            yield return (waiting, "cursor");
    }

    private List<int> Hints()
    {
        var cells = new List<int>(10);
        if (!Interactive || State.IsGameOver) return cells;

        // Eight was five before portals: a pawn on a mouth whose far end is occupied is
        // offered four plain steps and the far mouth's four free sides. Ten is what the
        // generator itself reserves, and a buffer narrower than what it may write is not a
        // hint missing from the board — it is a write past the end of the stack buffer.
        Span<Move> buffer = stackalloc Move[10];
        int count = State.GeneratePawnMoves(buffer);
        for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) cells.Add(buffer[i].Cell);

        return cells;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Both mouths of every portal, each carrying the pair it belongs to so the two ends of
    /// one can be drawn in one ink. Read straight off the position, which is where the
    /// pairs live, so nothing has to be handed to the board beside it the way the holes are.
    /// </summary>
    private List<(int Cell, int Pair)> Mouths()
    {
        var mouths = new List<(int, int)>(4);

        ulong pairs = State.Portals;
        int pair = 0;

        while (pairs != 0)
        {
            int low = System.Numerics.BitOperations.TrailingZeroCount(pairs);
            pairs &= pairs - 1;

            mouths.Add((low, pair));
            mouths.Add((GameState.PortalPartner(low), pair));
            pair++;
        }

        return mouths;
    }

    private IEnumerable<(MoveKind Kind, int Row, int Col, int Owner)> PlacedWalls()
    {
        for (int slot = 0; slot < Board.SlotCount; slot++)
        {
            int row = slot / Board.SlotSize;
            int col = slot % Board.SlotSize;
            int owner = State.WallOwner(slot);

            if ((State.HorizontalWalls & (1UL << slot)) != 0)
                yield return (MoveKind.HorizontalWall, row, col, owner);

            if ((State.VerticalWalls & (1UL << slot)) != 0)
                yield return (MoveKind.VerticalWall, row, col, owner);
        }
    }

    private List<(string Accent, string Points)> Routes()
    {
        var routes = new List<(string, string)>(2);
        if (State.IsGameOver) return routes;

        Span<byte> distances = stackalloc byte[Board.CellCount];

        for (int player = 0; player < 2; player++)
        {
            var cells = new List<int>(Board.CellCount);

            PathFinder.FillDistancesToGoal(State, player, distances);
            PathFinder.TraceShortestPath(State, player, distances, cells);

            if (cells.Count < 2) continue;

            string accent = player == 0 ? "p0" : "p1";

            // One ribbon per stretch that is actually walked, rather than one per route: a
            // route through a portal has two consecutive squares half a board apart, and a
            // ribbon laid between them would claim the pawn walks that ground — and passes
            // those walls — on the way home. It is cut at the mouth and picked up at the
            // far one, which is also what the route does.
            foreach (string run in Walked(cells)) routes.Add((accent, run));
        }

        return routes;
    }

    /// <summary>Each unbroken stretch of a route, as the polyline through its centres.</summary>
    private static IEnumerable<string> Walked(List<int> cells)
    {
        int start = 0;

        for (int i = 1; i <= cells.Count; i++)
        {
            if (i < cells.Count && AreNeighbours(cells[i - 1], cells[i])) continue;

            if (i - start >= 2) yield return Points(cells.GetRange(start, i - start));
            start = i;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>Whether two squares are a step apart on the board itself, portals aside.</summary>
    private static bool AreNeighbours(int a, int b) =>
        Math.Abs(Board.RowOf(a) - Board.RowOf(b)) + Math.Abs(Board.ColOf(a) - Board.ColOf(b)) == 1;

    /// <summary>A run of squares as the polyline through their centres.</summary>
    private static string Points(List<int> cells) =>
        string.Join(' ', cells.Select(c => $"{Centre(Board.ColOf(c))},{Centre(Board.RowOf(c))}"));

    // ==================================================================== reading ==

    /// <summary>
    /// How many steps every square is from a player's pawn, flooding out the way that pawn
    /// may actually walk. Squares out of play need no test of their own: sealing one blocks
    /// it in all four directions and blocks each of its neighbours back toward it, so a
    /// frontier can neither enter a gap nor leave one — and the ring outside a smaller
    /// board is sealed the same way, square by square, when the game is built.
    /// </summary>
    private static void FillReach(in GameState state, int player, Span<byte> steps)
    {
        steps.Fill(PathFinder.Unreachable);

        UInt128 reached = Board.Bit(state.PawnOf(player));
        UInt128 previous = 0;
        byte distance = 0;

        while (reached != previous)
        {
            UInt128 layer = reached & ~previous;

            while (layer != 0)
            {
                steps[Board.LowestBit(layer)] = distance;
                layer &= layer - UInt128.One;
            }

            previous = reached;
            reached = PathFinder.Expand(state, reached);
            distance++;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Works out the whole of the reading at once: which player reaches each square first,
    /// how far ahead they are there, and the line along which the answer changes.
    /// </summary>
    private void ReadPosition()
    {
        Span<byte> first = stackalloc byte[Board.CellCount];
        Span<byte> second = stackalloc byte[Board.CellCount];

        FillReach(State, 0, first);
        FillReach(State, 1, second);

        for (int cell = 0; cell < Board.CellCount; cell++)
        {
            byte mine = first[cell];
            byte theirs = second[cell];

            // Neither can get there — a pocket the walls closed off, or a square that is
            // not part of this game. It belongs to nobody and stays a plain tile.
            if (mine == PathFinder.Unreachable && theirs == PathFinder.Unreachable)
            {
                _owner[cell] = -1;
                continue;
            }

            // A square both pawns reach in the same number of steps goes to whoever is on
            // move, because they take the first of those steps. That is also why the seam
            // shifts by a square as the turn passes: the race has genuinely changed hands,
            // and under a toggle you have asked for that is the thing you asked to see.
            _owner[cell] = mine == theirs ? State.SideToMove : mine < theirs ? 0 : 1;

            int margin = mine == PathFinder.Unreachable || theirs == PathFinder.Unreachable
                ? Decisive
                : Math.Abs(mine - theirs);

            _stain[cell] = FaintestStain +
                (StrongestStain - FaintestStain) * Math.Min(margin, Decisive) / Decisive;
        }

        _front = BuildFront();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The seam, as one path: every groove with a different answer on either side of it.
    /// Drawn down the middle of the gap and a whole pitch long, so consecutive segments
    /// meet at the corners and the two edges of the field are where the line ends.
    /// </summary>
    private string BuildFront()
    {
        var path = new System.Text.StringBuilder();

        for (int row = From; row <= Last; row++)
        {
            for (int col = From; col <= Last; col++)
            {
                int cell = Board.Index(row, col);
                int owner = _owner[cell];
                if (owner < 0) continue;

                if (col < Last && Divides(owner, cell + 1))
                    path.Append($"M{Origin(col) + Cell + Gap / 2} {Origin(row) - Gap / 2}v{Pitch}");

                if (row < Last && Divides(owner, cell + Board.Size))
                    path.Append($"M{Origin(col) - Gap / 2} {Origin(row) + Cell + Gap / 2}h{Pitch}");
            }
        }

        return path.ToString();
    }

    /// <summary>Whether the seam runs between a square and the neighbour given.</summary>
    private bool Divides(int owner, int neighbour) => _owner[neighbour] >= 0 && _owner[neighbour] != owner;

    /// <summary>Which player's reach a square is in, as a class. Nothing outside the reading.</summary>
    private string Territory(int cell) =>
        !Reading || _owner[cell] < 0 ? string.Empty : _owner[cell] == 0 ? "reach p0" : "reach p1";

    /// <summary>How far that square is restained toward its owner, for the fill to mix by.</summary>
    private string? Stain(int cell) =>
        !Reading || _owner[cell] < 0 ? null : $"--stain: {_stain[cell]}%";

    // ===================================================================== detour ==

    /// <summary>
    /// What the wall under the pointer would do to the route it is aimed at: the stretch of
    /// the old way that dies, and the new way round. Both arcs are cut to the part that
    /// actually differs and extended one square past each end, so they leave and rejoin the
    /// same route rather than being two separate drawings of the whole board.
    ///
    /// The work is four flood fills and two walks of at most eighty-one squares, on a hover
    /// that already places the wall and measures both routes to write the sentence in the
    /// panel. The engine only takes the thread after a move, never during one.
    /// </summary>
    private (string Accent, string Gone, string Now, int Length)? Detour()
    {
        if (!Interactive || State.IsGameOver) return null;
        if (_pointerWall is not { } wall) return null;
        if (!State.IsWallLegal(wall.Kind, wall.Row, wall.Col)) return null;

        // The wall is played against the other player, so theirs is the route it bends.
        int player = State.Opponent;

        Span<byte> distances = stackalloc byte[Board.CellCount];

        var before = new List<int>(Board.CellCount);
        PathFinder.FillDistancesToGoal(State, player, distances);
        PathFinder.TraceShortestPath(State, player, distances, before);
        if (before.Count < 2) return null;

        GameState placed = State;
        placed.PlaceWallUnchecked(wall.Kind, wall.Row, wall.Col);

        UInt128 old = 0;
        foreach (int cell in before) old |= Board.Bit(cell);

        var after = new List<int>(Board.CellCount);
        PathFinder.FillDistancesToGoal(placed, player, distances);
        Trace(placed, player, distances, old, after);
        if (after.Count < 2) return null;

        UInt128 taken = 0;
        foreach (int cell in after) taken |= Board.Bit(cell);

        // Cut at the first portal the stretch takes rather than broken into pieces at each
        // one: unlike a ribbon, the new way round is a single line that draws itself in,
        // and both the drawing and the length it is measured in assume one unbroken run.
        // What is kept is the part nearest the pawn, which is the part being asked about.
        List<int> gone = Unbroken(Stretch(before, taken));
        List<int> now = Unbroken(Stretch(after, old));

        // The wall bends nothing this player was going to do anyway. The panel still says
        // what it costs; there is simply no detour to draw.
        if (gone.Count < 2 && now.Count < 2) return null;

        return (player == 0 ? "p0" : "p1",
            gone.Count < 2 ? string.Empty : Points(gone),
            now.Count < 2 ? string.Empty : Points(now),
            (now.Count - 1) * Pitch);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// One shortest route, preferring squares the other route already used. Ties on a
    /// distance field are everywhere and choosing among them arbitrarily redraws a route
    /// that has not really changed; keeping to the old one wherever it is still shortest is
    /// the whole of why the difference reads as a detour rather than as a new board.
    /// </summary>
    private static void Trace(in GameState state, int player, Span<byte> distances, UInt128 old, List<int> cells)
    {
        int cell = state.PawnOf(player);
        if (distances[cell] == PathFinder.Unreachable) return;

        cells.Add(cell);

        while (distances[cell] > 0)
        {
            int next = -1;
            bool kept = false;

            for (int dir = 0; dir < 4; dir++)
            {
                if (state.Blocked(cell, dir)) continue;

                int neighbour = cell + Board.Delta[dir];
                byte step = distances[neighbour];

                // On a distance field anything strictly closer is already one step closer,
                // so this is the whole of the test for being on a shortest route.
                if (step == PathFinder.Unreachable || step >= distances[cell]) continue;

                bool familiar = (old & Board.Bit(neighbour)) != 0;

                // Take the first candidate, and after that only trade up: a square the old
                // route used, in place of one it did not.
                if (next >= 0 && !(familiar && !kept)) continue;

                next = neighbour;
                kept = familiar;
            }

            if (next < 0) break;

            cell = next;
            cells.Add(cell);
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The run of a route that the other one does not have, with one square kept at each
    /// end so the two arcs start and finish on the same squares — which is what makes them
    /// read as one route leaving and rejoining rather than as two unrelated lines.
    /// </summary>
    private static List<int> Stretch(List<int> route, UInt128 shared)
    {
        int first = -1;
        int last = -1;

        for (int i = 0; i < route.Count; i++)
        {
            if ((shared & Board.Bit(route[i])) != 0) continue;

            if (first < 0) first = i;
            last = i;
        }

        if (first < 0) return new List<int>();

        int from = Math.Max(first - 1, 0);
        int to = Math.Min(last + 1, route.Count - 1);

        return route.GetRange(from, to - from + 1);
    }

    /// <summary>The leading part of a route, up to the first portal it takes.</summary>
    private static List<int> Unbroken(List<int> route)
    {
        for (int i = 1; i < route.Count; i++)
            if (!AreNeighbours(route[i - 1], route[i]))
                return route.GetRange(0, i);

        return route;
    }

    // =================================================================== geometry ==

    /// <summary>
    /// Where along the finish line the winning pawn crossed it, for the flood to start
    /// from. A percentage rather than a coordinate because the transform is taken in the
    /// bar's own box, and the bar is the whole row wide however big the board is. Written
    /// invariantly: this is CSS, and a browser set to a comma decimal point would be
    /// handed a declaration it can only drop.
    /// </summary>
    private string Crossing(int player)
    {
        int span = Span * Cell + (Span - 1) * Gap;
        double at = 100.0 * (Centre(Board.ColOf(State.PawnOf(player))) - Origin(From)) / span;

        return FormattableString.Invariant($"transform-origin: {at:0.#}% 50%");
    }

    private static int Origin(int index) => Pad + index * Pitch;

    private static int Centre(int index) => Pad + index * Pitch + Cell / 2;

    private static int WallX(MoveKind kind, int col) =>
        kind == MoveKind.HorizontalWall ? Origin(col) : Origin(col) + Cell;

    private static int WallY(MoveKind kind, int row) =>
        kind == MoveKind.HorizontalWall ? Origin(row) + Cell : Origin(row);

    private static int LastX(Move move) =>
        move.Kind == MoveKind.Pawn ? Origin(move.Col) : WallX(move.Kind, move.Col);

    private static int LastY(Move move) =>
        move.Kind == MoveKind.Pawn ? Origin(move.Row) : WallY(move.Kind, move.Row);

    private static int LastWidth(Move move) => move.Kind switch
    {
        MoveKind.Pawn => Cell,
        MoveKind.HorizontalWall => WallLength,
        _ => Gap,
    };

    private static int LastHeight(Move move) => move.Kind switch
    {
        MoveKind.Pawn => Cell,
        MoveKind.HorizontalWall => Gap,
        _ => WallLength,
    };
}
